  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 130 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nUpper-level northwesterly flow develops on the backside of the \ndeparting upper-level trough for Sunday as the surface low \nslowly treks more eastward across the Eastern Great Lakes. Still\nexpecting the tighter pressure gradient to linger across \nsouthern WI and will see another day of gusty north- \nnortheasterly. High swim risk and dangerous currents will \ncontinue during this time as well. Thus Beach Hazard Statement \nremains in effect until Sunday evening. Outside of a small \npotential for wrap around scattered showers, Sunday is looking \nprimarily dry across much of the CWA as high pressure begins to \nbuild down across the Upper Midwest. Temps will be slightly \nwarmer given less cloud cover with high in the mid to upper 70s \nand overnight lows falling into 50s.\n\nStart of next week will remain fairly quiet and dry given the \nhigh pressure sitting over the Upper Great Lakes region with \nnear normal temps. Daytime temps in the 80s and overnight lows \nin the upper 50s into the 60s. Surface high eventually moves \neastward later Monday into Tuesday allowing for southerly flow \nand WAA to gradually build into southern WI. Will be a few \ndegrees warmer on Tuesday with increasing dewpoints and PWATs \nabove 1 inch as an upper- level trough digs across the Upper \nMidwest. The main mid-level vorticity lobe is progged to stay to\nthe north along with the associated surface low. However, this \nsystems cold front is then expected to drag across our neck of \nthe woods later Tuesday into Wednesday. Given the forcing along \nthe front paired with the increasing moisture, expecting \nincreased precip for midweek. However, uncertainty remains on \nthe timing of the cold front. Nevertheless, mid-range models are\nhinting at some instability increasing ahead of the front and \nenough to support increased thunderstorm chances.\n\nOtherwise the pattern is trending more active for the end of \nthe week as long range models hint at upper-level waves passing \nthrough the region. However, uncertainty remains given the \ntiming differences and various model solutions this far out. \n\nWagner",
